Encapsulation

Encapsulation means wrapping up the implementation of data members and methods together in a single unit (class) and controlling their access.
It is mainly about data hiding using access modifiers.




Abstraction

Abstraction means hiding unnecessary details and showing only the important and necessary information to the user.
Example:
When we use System.out.println("Hello");, we don’t know the complex internal implementation — we only know it prints text.




Key Difference

Encapsulation deals with internal implementation — how data and methods are organized and protected inside a class.
Abstraction deals with design problems — deciding which details to show to the outside world and which to hide.


